  "Number": 6,
  "Question": "The smallest value of \\(x^2+8x\\) for real values of \\(x\\) is:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(-16.25\\)",
    "B": "\\(-16\\)",
    "C": "\\(-15\\)",
    "D": "\\(-8\\)",
    "E": "None of these"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Solution": "A real square is never negative, so rewriting the expression as a square plus a constant will reveal its minimum. The middle term \\(8x=2\\cdot4\\cdot x\\) suggests adding and subtracting \\(4^2=16\\):\n\\[\nx^2+8x=(x^2+8x+16)-16=(x+4)^2-16.\n\\]\nSince \\((x+4)^2\\ge0\\) for every real \\(x\\), the expression is at least \\(-16\\). This bound is attained at \\(x=-4\\), where the square equals zero. The smallest value is therefore \\(\\boxed{-16}\\), choice B.",
